VERDICT: FAIL
CHECKER: checker-second
FAMILY: adversarial
TASK: R1
ATTEMPT: 2
---
Scope: internal/services/accounts/accounts.go, internal/handlers/accounts/handlers.go,
internal/services/mcpsvc/ledger/anchor.go, internal/services/mcpsvc/ledger/register.go
(.swarm/manifests/R1.2.files), merged wt-primary per .swarm/tier3/R1/report.md RESOLUTION.

CONFIRMED CORRECT (do not re-litigate):
- accept.sh passes end-to-end: build, vet, pinned Mutate signature, no unserialized
  save left at the four call sites, oracle tests (-race) pass, full suite (-race) passes.
- Mutate's lock order matches storage.go's contract: package `mu` taken outside
  BeginShared, SharedTx.ReadFile/WriteFile bypass dataMu re-entry correctly
  (verified Storage.ReadFile itself takes no dataMu lock, so no recursive-RLock hazard).
- ensureSnapshot (anchor.go) runs before Mutate opens, never inside the closure —
  no cross-service call under the held section.
- No closure body (handleDelete/handleAddAnchor/handleDeleteAnchor/applyForm/
  applyAnchor/register.go adapter) calls back into accounts.Load/Save or another
  service; grepped and read every fn body in scope.
- Own concurrency test: 8 goroutines/account x 20 mutations across 8 DIFFERENT
  accounts, plus 4 concurrent plain-Load readers throughout — zero lost writes,
  zero torn/short reads (go test -race, reproduced independently of the oracle's
  single-account 32-goroutine case).
- register.go's storageAccountStore.SaveAccounts (the seam the task flagged as
  highest-value): confirmed via grep that it has ZERO production callers anywhere
  in the tree, so its blind-overwrite-inside-Mutate shape (which I reproduced can
  lose a concurrent Mutate write if composed with LoadAccounts by a future caller)
  does not currently falsify the "no lost writes" claim for any reachable code
  path. This is a latent design footgun worth naming, not a live regression — not
  the basis for this FAIL.
- applyForm's returned `result` is the same slice passed into saveTx (verified
  saveTx does not mutate elements), so callers still get post-computation state
  consistent with what old accounts.Save-based code returned.

REAL DEFECT — violates "no observable behaviour changed":

1. HTTP status-code regression on 3 of the 4 required call sites (handleDelete,
   handleAddAnchor, handleDeleteAnchor). Pre-merge (git show HEAD), a Load
   failure in these three handlers hit a distinct branch:
     accts, err := accounts.Load(store)
     if err != nil {
         renderError(w, "Failed to load accounts: "+err.Error(), http.StatusInternalServerError)
         return
     }
   i.e. HTTP 500 with a "Failed to load accounts: ..." message, DISTINCT from
   the save-failure branch (renderList/200 with data.Error).
   After the merge, load and save are one Mutate closure, so a load failure
   and a save failure are indistinguishable to the handler: both fall through
   to the generic `data.Error = err.Error(); renderList(w, r, data)` branch,
   which is HTTP 200.
   Reproduced empirically (test written, run, and deleted after use — not left
   in the tree): corrupted accounts.json, then POST /accounts/{id}/anchor via
   handleAddAnchor through the real router (internal/handlers/accounts,
   TestZZ_AddAnchorOnLoadFailure_StatusCode style harness using existing
   setupTestEnv/newRouter/formPost helpers):
     status=200 body={... "Error":"invalid accounts file: invalid character 'n' ..." ...}
   Old code would have returned 500 via renderError with a "Failed to load
   accounts: " prefix instead. Same shape applies to handleDelete and
   handleDeleteAnchor (identical pre-merge pattern, confirmed by reading
   git show HEAD for both). applyForm's callers (handleCreate/handleUpdate)
   are NOT affected — they already collapsed Load and Save errors to the same
   200/renderList path pre-merge, so no regression there.
   This is exactly the class of defect the assignment asked me to hunt for
   ("a 500 where there used to be a 404, or vice versa") — here it is 500
   silently becoming 200 on a genuine backend failure (corrupt sidecar, or a
   locked/encrypted store not yet unlocked, which routes through the same
   accounts.Load error path). A client that checks response.ok / status code
   (including HTMX's default error handling, which budget2 uses elsewhere)
   would treat this failure as success.

2. Secondary, same category as the lead's own rejection reason for wt-alt
   (dropped OverlapWarnings): accounts.go's saveTx logs OverlapWarnings only
   AFTER a successful tx.WriteFile (lines ~120-139); on a WriteFile failure it
   returns early and never logs the already-computed warnings. The pre-merge
   Save() (`warnings, err := SaveWithWarnings(...); for _, w := range warnings
   { log.Printf(...) }`) logs those same warnings UNCONDITIONALLY, even when
   the underlying write fails. So a write failure that coincides with an
   overlap warning now produces fewer log lines than before. Narrower and
   lower-severity than #1 (server-log-only, requires two simultaneous
   conditions), but it is the same "silent side-effect drop on the save path"
   category the lead flagged as disqualifying for the other implementation,
   and it survives in the accepted one.

3. Minor, error-text-only: anchor.go's applyAnchor previously produced a
   distinct message for a LoadAccounts failure ("cannot reload accounts
   before writing: %w") versus a SaveAccounts failure (wrapped with snapNote
   and "the confirmation token has been spent either way..."). Post-merge,
   both are folded into the single Mutate-error branch and always get the
   snapNote-wrapped "token has been spent" wording, even for a bare load
   failure where the write never even started. Confirmed via diff against
   git show HEAD. Text-only (still an error, same category), listed for
   completeness since the assignment asked specifically about error strings
   on this path.

CONCLUSION: the concurrency and restore-resurrection claims hold up under
adversarial testing (cross-account races, reader/writer races, lock-order
review). The third clause of the claim under test — "no observable behaviour
changed" — does not hold: finding #1 is a reproducible HTTP status-code and
response-shape regression on 3 of the 4 required call sites, not a hypothetical.
Default-to-FAIL threshold is met by #1 alone; #2 and #3 corroborate that the
save/error paths were not diffed carefully enough against git show HEAD before
merge, matching the exact failure mode the lead already caught once in the
rejected sibling implementation.
